course: dictionary
lesson: 2014
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one. The word conduct can mean to lead music, to carry out a test, to let heat or electricity pass through, or how someone behaves. «Metals conduct heat well, so a metal spoon in hot soup gets hot.» The spoon gets hot, so conduct here means to let heat pass through.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The others promised to be answerable for his good conduct» is about behaviour, because the sentence talks about promising to behave. Conduct is a noun here, a thing you have, and it is stressed on CON-, as in CON-duct.
explain: In science, conduct is a verb: «Water with impurities can conduct electricity.» That means water lets electricity pass through it. So «Dry your hands before touching plugs or switches, because water conducts electricity into your body.»
explain: Try the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the word. If "behaviour" fits in «good conduct», you have it. If "let pass through" fits in «conducts electricity», you have that meaning too.
fact: As a verb, conduct can mean to lead, to carry out, or to let something pass through: conduct an experiment, conduct electricity.
fact: As a noun, conduct means behaviour: good conduct, or a code of conduct, which is a set of rules.
fact: Materials that conduct heat or electricity are called conductors, such as metals like copper.
fact: Materials that do not conduct heat or electricity are called insulators, such as plastic, wood and rubber.
fact: Metal feels colder than wood at the same temperature because it conducts heat away from your skin faster.
exercise: «Metals conduct heat well, so a metal spoon in hot soup gets hot.» What does conduct mean here? | To behave politely | *To let heat pass through | To guide a tour group | To lead a band with a baton
exercise: «The others promised to be answerable for his good conduct.» What does conduct mean in this sentence? | To organise a survey | *Behaviour, how someone acts | To lead music | To carry electricity into a plug
exercise: In science, what do we call a material that does not conduct heat or electricity? | Experiment | Conductor | *Insulator | Orchestra
test: Which phrase means to carry out an experiment in science? | Conduct a tour of the museum | *Conduct an experiment | Code of conduct | Good conduct
test: «Dry your hands before touching plugs or switches, because water conducts electricity into your body.» Why should you dry your hands? | Water makes plugs look dirty | Water stops electricity from passing through | *Water can carry electricity into your body | Water makes your hands colder
exam: In «Metal feels colder than wood at the same temperature because it conducts heat away from your skin faster», what does conducts mean? | Behaves well | *Lets heat pass through | Leads a band | Sings loudly
